<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Karystos</h1><p class="st-meta">800 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.) · capital Karystos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">City of southern Euboea, forced into the Delian League by Athens and later famed for its green-veined Cipollino marble.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Karystos lay at the southern end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>, below <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-ochi">Mount Ochi</a>. Greek tradition held that its people were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dryopes">Dryopes</a>, later joined by Ionian settlers. It grew in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> as a farming and seafaring community, controlling the channel between Euboea and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/andros">Andros</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> forced Karystos to submit in 490 BCE, and it fought on their side in 480 BCE. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> then compelled it to join the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delian-league">Delian League</a> c. 472 BCE. In the 4th century BCE it produced the physician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocles-of-carystus">Diocles</a>, and later the writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a>. Its green-veined marble, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cipollino-marble">Cipollino</a>, was quarried heavily in Roman times.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Karystos passed between Macedonian kings and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> and came under Roman control by 146 BCE. Its marble quarries remained important under the Empire, and the modern town keeps the ancient name.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 800 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:43.78%;width:13.01%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Karystos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ionic Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Carystus</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-473&amp;focus=karystos" aria-label="Karystos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5289 811 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Karystos lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5609 1067L5591 1068M5609 1067L5591 1068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5591 1068h0M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5609 1067h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5609 1067h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-473&amp;focus=karystos">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

